# Captured adapter source identity (issue 131)

Implementation base: `5033124b5d6d4c566a8967e6c7369a5d01672246`.
The unchanged independent test from `c9f611b96` has Git blob
`f7f130bcb5a4506e471136ca8cd45aba81032543`. Both controls fail before
this repair: hidden default-index source edits and an external executed entry
receive a qualified candidate pass. The retained binary stderr proves the changed
adapter code ran. Raw baseline: `.runtime/adapter123/source131-before.tap`.

## Cause and correction

`run.mjs` previously treated `git status` in the manifest's working directory as
source identity. Index visibility flags hide bytes from that observation; the
working directory can also differ from the executed source entry. The grader
trusted that commit/dirty pair without executed-source evidence.

The new observer extracts the raw-byte Git blob comparison from root repair
`95b1e9571da82ac41a2f840e7ed066b53c1b8480` into an independent runner
module. Root release-checker files and `source-git-environment.mjs` are unchanged.
The complete committed inventory is combined with index/untracked inventory, and
each regular file is read directly and compared to its committed Git blob. A
second observation follows owned process closure. Evidence includes the raw
commit object, file modes, paths, byte counts, SHA-256 and actual/expected blob
identities. The grader reconstructs the complete Git tree and checks its binding
to the externally pinned commit, rather than trusting a stored qualification flag.

A direct Node file recipe receives an explicit `--import` module witness. The
effective command uses the runner's Node executable and records its hash/version,
the observer hash, requested/effective commands, entry identity and owned PID.
Synchronous module hooks return the original module source unchanged and record
loaded file bytes, including dynamic file imports. The grader correlates the
witness PID with the owned process and compares the actual entry and every
witnessed file module to the committed inventory. Temporarily changing a loaded
dependency and restoring it before the final source observation does not pass.

Captures still run when source is dirty, missing or unsupported. Existing raw
stdout/stderr retention, exit/interruption provenance, protocol, result inventory,
normalization, explicit candidate pins and diagnostic comparisons remain in
place. Unqualified captures cannot produce a candidate CLI success.

## Supported limits

The initial `374f99d2b` witness supports direct Node file entries. Shell, eval/preload
recipes, inherited `NODE_OPTIONS`, non-file or transformed module sources,
external/untracked dependencies and incomplete witnesses do not qualify.
Loading child-process, worker, VM or custom-module-loader interfaces is
conservatively unqualified because their secondary execution is not witnessed.
Ruby and other unsupported recipes still capture observations; they need their
own executed-source proof before candidate qualification. At that phase the
TypeScript adapter's custom loader was not accepted by the limited witness.
The subsequent canonical-loader work is recorded below; neither phase
establishes every backend or the complete port.

This observer is not a sandbox or a hostile-engine attestation. It observes
Node's module-loading path, not every possible use of dynamically generated
code or external data. Capture evidence still needs normal custody and review;
a malicious program able to rewrite its evidence is outside this proof. Module
hooks were checked against the official Node module API and exercised with the
installed Node runtime: https://nodejs.org/api/module.html#customization-hooks.

