## Canonical loader and generated-source followup

Followup base: `ba47ae86b9fba32a5b2c51fedad008e8692f35b6`.
The supported additional recipe is the unchanged canonical
`reference-harness/adapters/ts/adapter.mjs` registering the exact recognized
`tools/oracle/typescript-source-loader.mjs`. No arbitrary loader is admitted by
its filename. The entry/loader/helper must match known instrument bytes in the
candidate tree, and the actual registration must be the expected call. An
observer installed before it records loader/toolchain module source actually
consumed in its worker. Node's asynchronous CommonJS bridge can expose null
source; the worker also observes the actual content argument delivered to
`Module.prototype._compile`, rather than substituting a later disk hash.

The real loader emits opt-in receipts at the `transformWithOxc` call/result seam.
They identify its exact input string, URL, input Git object/SHA-256, actual
returned JavaScript and transformer-function identity. The main module witness
then identifies the output Node consumed. A cache hit still executes the
existing cached program; a fresh transform separately checks its bytes. A
poisoned cache or temporarily changed input stays unqualified while the program
runs. Shared counters record observed/retained worker events, transform receipts
and errors. A lost receipt cannot become acceptable when its journal is later
restored. Main-audit publication is atomic; failed publication retains real
errors and an incomplete primary checkpoint.

Installed runtime/toolchain dependencies are separate from candidate Git source.
Actual module content and installed manifests are recorded, with name/version
admission against the committed dependency lock. This identifies the installed
artifacts used; it does not authenticate registry publishers or prove a local
installation equals a registry archive. Unknown external entries, undeclared
dependencies, extra loaders and unwitnessed secondary execution stay unqualified.
Builtin lookup and the child-process prototype path are observed; this recipe
does not simply exempt an import of `node:module` from scrutiny.

